<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · city</p><h1 class="enc-title">Artvin</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Artvin is a city in north-eastern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/turkey">Turkey</a>, the capital of Artvin Province, on the Çoruh river near the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/black-sea">Black Sea</a> and the Georgian border. Part of the medieval Georgian lands of Tao-<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/klarjeti">Klarjeti</a>, it passed to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ottoman-empire">Ottoman Empire</a> in the 16th century and was annexed by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/russia">Russia</a> in 1878. It returned to Turkey under the Treaty of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kars">Kars</a> in 1921.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/artvin.jpg" alt="Artvin" width="320" height="213" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Cagriaksu25 · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:A%C3%87%C3%9C_%C5%9Eehir_Yerle%C5%9Fkesi.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Setting</h2><p>Artvin stands on a hill overlooking the Çoruh River near the Deriner Dam, about 30 kilometres inland from the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/black-sea">Black Sea</a>. Above the town is a fortress that once lay within the medieval Georgian district of Nigali, with a single circuit wall around the summit, several rooms and a large donjon at the west.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>Artifacts from the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bronze-age">Bronze Age</a> and earlier have been found. The area was part of Colchis and of Greater Armenia, and was invaded first by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/scythians">Scythians</a> and then by Muslim armies led by Habib, son of Caliph Uthman, who held it from 853 CE until the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/byzantine-empire">Byzantines</a> conquered it in 1023. The Principalities of Tao-<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/klarjeti">Klarjeti</a> arose in the 7th and 8th centuries and made the region one of the most important religious centres of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/georgia">Georgia</a>. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/seljuk-empire">Seljuk Turks</a> conquered it in 1064, and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/safavid-empire">Safavids</a> took it in 1502.</p><p>The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ottoman-empire">Ottomans</a> under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mehmed-ii">Mehmed II</a> defeated the Empire of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/trabzon">Trebizond</a> and brought the eastern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/black-sea">Black Sea</a> coast and its mountain hinterland under their control, and on 13 July 1551 their hold on Artvin was secure. After about 250 years the area was ceded to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/russia">Russia</a> following the war of 1828 to 1829, recovered, and ceded again after the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/russo-turkish-war-1877-1878">Russo-Turkish War</a> of 1877 to 1878. Much of the largely Muslim Georgian population moved west. British troops arrived in 1918, followed by the Democratic Republic of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/georgia">Georgia</a>, and the Treaty of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kars">Kars</a> in 1921 ceded Artvin to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/turkey">Turkey</a>.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">The place today</h2><p>Artvin is the seat of Artvin Province and Artvin District, with 25,841 people in 2021, and the home of Artvin Çoruh University. The castle, also called Livane, was built in 937, and Ottoman buildings include the Salih Bey mosque of 1792 and the fountain of Çelebi Efendi of 1783. The Camili (Macahel) area is a UNESCO Biosphere Reserve. Artvin is also a former Armenian Catholic see, established in 1850 and listed as a titular see since 1972.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Artvin” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>On 7 June 1945 Vyacheslav Molotov told the Turkish ambassador that Kars, Ardahan, <b>Artvin</b> and Surmalu should be returned to the Soviet Union in the name of Armenia and Georgia, a claim backed by the Catholicos and every part of the diaspora, the Dashnaksutiun included.</blockquote><a href="/empire/armenian-ssr">Armenian SSR · Military</a></li><li><blockquote>Governed from Akhaltsikhe, the eyalet included Ardahan, <b>Artvin</b> and the upper Kura valleys.</blockquote><a href="/state/childir-eyalet">Childir Eyalet</a></li></ul></section>
